{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NQcgrwKvDJkqanGP8Fb6wP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# PROJECT_MAP.md — Directory Layout\n2\t\n3\t```\n4\tAGENTS.md          — Workspace orientation and rules\n5\tPROJECT_MAP.md     — This file; directory summary\n6\tRULEBOOK.txt       — The entire MTG comprehensive rules. Grep — do not read whole.\n7\tprompt.md          — Per-run task prompt (written at stage time)\n8\trun_manifest.json  — Per-run manifest written by the harness: `{timeout_seconds, deadline_utc}`. Harness-owned — do not edit.\n9\tpytest.ini         — Pytest configuration for the workspace\n10\tconftest.py        — Pytest fixtures shared across the workspace\n11\ttest_utils.py      — Shared test helpers (`create_game`, `set_board_state`, …)\n12\ttest_utils.md      — API reference for `test_utils.py`\n13\t.gitignore         — Git ignore rules\n14\tengine/            — Canonical game engine source. Imported as `engine`.\n15\tengine_tests/      — Engine regression tests (do not modify).\n16\tcards/             — Card implementations.\n17\t  cards/fdn/       — Completed FDN reference cards (do not modify their tests).\n18\t  cards/sos/       — SOS card stubs to implement.\n19\tskills/            — Workspace-local skills (e.g. `grep-rulebook/SKILL.md`).\n20\t```\n21\t\n22\t## Card paths\n23\t\n24\tFor an SOS card with collector number `N`:\n25\t\n26\t```\n27\tcards/sos/sos_<N>/card_spec.json   — card metadata (name, mana_cost, oracle_text, P/T, keywords, …)\n28\tcards/sos/sos_<N>/card_impl.py     — implementation stub you complete\n29\tcards/sos/sos_<N>/tests.py         — your tests for this card (you create this)\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\tFor an FDN reference card:\n33\t\n34\t```\n35\tcards/fdn/fdn_<N>/card_spec.json   — card metadata\n36\tcards/fdn/fdn_<N>/card_impl.py     — completed reference implementation (read for examples)\n37\tcards/fdn/fdn_<N>/tests.py         — only present for the cards listed below; read as test examples\n38\t```\n39\t\n40\t### FDN cards that ship with a `tests.py`\n41\t\n42\tThis is the canonical list — agent prompts refer back here instead of duplicating it.\n43\tDiscover the current set at any time with:\n44\t\n45\t```bash\n46\tfind cards/fdn -mindepth 2 -maxdepth 2 -name tests.py -printf '%h\\n' | sort -V\n47\t```\n48\t\n49\tAs of the latest workspace stage, the list is: `fdn_13`, `fdn_142`, `fdn_205`, `fdn_215`, `fdn_244`. If you suspect the list has grown, re-run the `find` command above — it is authoritative.\n50\t\n51\t## Imports\n52\t\n53\tThe workspace root is on `sys.path`, so use bare package imports:\n54\t\n55\t```python\n56\tfrom cards.sos.sos_1.card_impl import TheDawningArchaic\n57\tfrom engine.card import CardImpl, Creature, Instant\n58\tfrom engine.types import CardType, Keyword, ManaCost, ManaType, Zone\n59\tfrom test_utils import create_game, set_board_state\n60\t```\n61\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"75cf876e-0070-4da1-a9bb-823668fe208a","uuid":"02176d91-e2e5-41cb-8bb4-d5a020f95383","timestamp":"2026-06-10T07:08:46.769Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/PROJECT_MAP.md","content":"# PROJECT_MAP.md — Directory Layout\n\n```\nAGENTS.md          — Workspace orientation and rules\nPROJECT_MAP.md     — This file; directory summary\nRULEBOOK.txt       — The entire MTG comprehensive rules. Grep — do not read whole.\nprompt.md          — Per-run task prompt (written at stage time)\nrun_manifest.json  — Per-run manifest written by the harness: `{timeout_seconds, deadline_utc}`. Harness-owned — do not edit.\npytest.ini         — Pytest configuration for the workspace\nconftest.py        — Pytest fixtures shared across the workspace\ntest_utils.py      — Shared test helpers (`create_game`, `set_board_state`, …)\ntest_utils.md      — API reference for `test_utils.py`\n.gitignore         — Git ignore rules\nengine/            — Canonical game engine source. Imported as `engine`.\nengine_tests/      — Engine regression tests (do not modify).\ncards/             — Card implementations.\n  cards/fdn/       — Completed FDN reference cards (do not modify their tests).\n  cards/sos/       — SOS card stubs to implement.\nskills/            — Workspace-local skills (e.g. `grep-rulebook/SKILL.md`).\n```\n\n## Card paths\n\nFor an SOS card with collector number `N`:\n\n```\ncards/sos/sos_<N>/card_spec.json   — card metadata (name, mana_cost, oracle_text, P/T, keywords, …)\ncards/sos/sos_<N>/card_impl.py     — implementation stub you complete\ncards/sos/sos_<N>/tests.py         — your tests for this card (you create this)\n```\n\nFor an FDN reference card:\n\n```\ncards/fdn/fdn_<N>/card_spec.json   — card metadata\ncards/fdn/fdn_<N>/card_impl.py     — completed reference implementation (read for examples)\ncards/fdn/fdn_<N>/tests.py         — only present for the cards listed below; read as test examples\n```\n\n### FDN cards that ship with a `tests.py`\n\nThis is the canonical list — agent prompts refer back here instead of duplicating it.\nDiscover the current set at any time with:\n\n```bash\nfind cards/fdn -mindepth 2 -maxdepth 2 -name tests.py -printf '%h\\n' | sort -V\n```\n\nAs of the latest workspace stage, the list is: `fdn_13`, `fdn_142`, `fdn_205`, `fdn_215`, `fdn_244`. If you suspect the list has grown, re-run the `find` command above — it is authoritative.\n\n## Imports\n\nThe workspace root is on `sys.path`, so use bare package imports:\n\n```python\nfrom cards.sos.sos_1.card_impl import TheDawningArchaic\nfrom engine.card import CardImpl, Creature, Instant\nfrom engine.types import CardType, Keyword, ManaCost, ManaType, Zone\nfrom test_utils import create_game, set_board_state\n```\n","numLines":61,"startLine":1,"totalLines":61}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NFt5JDHJ6zHVNwmPe7fQJ9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: grep-rulebook\n3\tdescription: Look up Magic: The Gathering rules in /workspace/RULEBOOK.txt using grep. Use whenever you're unsure how a keyword, timing rule, replacement effect, state-based action, or other mechanic actually works — before guessing in a card_impl.py or engine/ change.\n4\t---\n5\t\n6\t`RULEBOOK.txt` (workspace root) is the Magic: The Gathering Comprehensive Rules — the authoritative source for any rules question (keyword behavior, timing, replacement vs trigger ordering, state-based actions, etc.). **Whenever you are unsure how a mechanic works, check the rulebook before guessing.** A wrong card implementation costs you a card; an incorrect-by-design helper in `engine/` can fail many cards at once.\n7\t\n8\t## Read with grep, not cat / Read\n9\t\n10\t`RULEBOOK.txt` is very large. Reading the whole file blows your context budget and returns mostly noise. **Always grep.** Use `Read` only to expand a specific line range you've located with grep.\n11\t\n12\t## File structure (so you know what to grep for)\n13\t\n14\tThe file has two halves:\n15\t\n16\t1. **Numbered rules** (lines ~13–3,860) — hierarchical: section (e.g. `700.`), rule (e.g. `702.2`), subrule (e.g. `702.2a`). Common sections you'll hit a lot:\n17\t   - `100.`–`199.` — game concepts (mana, damage, counters, priority)\n18\t   - `300.`–`399.` — card types (Creature, Instant, Sorcery, Enchantment, Land, Artifact, Planeswalker)\n19\t   - `500.`–`599.` — turn structure (phases, steps, untap, upkeep, combat)\n20\t   - `600.`–`699.` — spells and abilities (casting, resolving, targeting, replacement effects)\n21\t   - `701.` — keyword actions (counter, destroy, exile, sacrifice, regenerate, …)\n22\t   - `702.` — keyword abilities (flying, trample, lifelink, deathtouch, ward, …)\n23\t   - `704.` — state-based actions\n24\t2. **Glossary** (lines ~3,861 onward) — alphabetical term definitions. Often the fastest path for \"what does X mean?\"\n25\t\n26\t## Grep recipes\n27\t\n28\t```bash\n29\t# 1. Look up a keyword ability by name\n30\t#    702. is the keyword-ability section. Most keywords get one numbered subrule.\n31\tgrep -n \"^702\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt | grep -i \"deathtouch\"\n32\t# → 3877:702.2. Deathtouch\n33\t# Then expand:\n34\tgrep -n -A 20 \"^702\\.2\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt | head -30\n35\t\n36\t# 2. Pull a specific rule + its subrules with context\n37\tgrep -n -A 30 \"^704\\.5\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt        # state-based-action enumeration\n38\tgrep -n -A 50 \"^601\\.2\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt        # casting-a-spell steps\n39\t\n40\t# 3. Glossary lookup (case-insensitive, anchored on a line that starts with the term)\n41\t#    Note: only proper keyword abilities have glossary entries. \"Ability words\"\n42\t#    (converge, landfall, magecraft, …) intentionally don't — they're flavor\n43\t#    tags with no rules text. If ^term returns nothing, fall through to (4).\n44\tgrep -n -i \"^deathtouch\" RULEBOOK.txt\n45\tgrep -n -i \"^lifelink\" RULEBOOK.txt\n46\t\n47\t# 4. Find every mention of a mechanic\n48\tgrep -ni \"replacement effect\" RULEBOOK.txt | head -20\n49\tgrep -ni \"ward\" RULEBOOK.txt | head -20\n50\t\n51\t# 5. Find which numbered rule discusses a topic, then drill in\n52\tgrep -n -E \"^[0-9]{3}\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt | grep -i \"combat\"\n53\t# → e.g. 506. Combat Phase\n54\tgrep -n -A 80 \"^506\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt\n55\t```\n56\t\n57\tOnce you've narrowed to a line range, switch to `Read` with `offset` + `limit` to pull just that block (cheaper than re-grepping).\n58\t\n59\t## Best practices\n60\t\n61\t- **Anchor with `^`** on rule-number lookups (`^702\\.`, `^704\\.5\\.`). Without the anchor you'll match every cross-reference to those rules elsewhere in the doc.\n62\t- **Escape the dot** in rule numbers (`702\\.2\\.`) — grep treats `.` as any char. With BRE this matches anyway, but the explicit escape is unambiguous.\n63\t- **Start narrow, then widen.** Try the specific rule number first (`702.19` for Trample). If you don't know the number, search the glossary (`^trample`). If neither hits, search the body with `-i`.\n64\t- **Use `-A` / `-B` for context.** Rules are paragraph-shaped; `-A 20` typically captures a rule plus its first few subrules without overflowing context.\n65\t- **Cap the output with `| head -N`** on broad searches — `grep -i \"creature\"` returns hundreds of hits.\n66\t- **Cross-reference, don't reinvent.** Card rules text often paraphrases the rulebook; when in doubt, the numbered rule wins over your reading of the card.\n67\t\n68\t## When NOT to consult the rulebook\n69\t\n70\t- The behavior is already encoded in `engine/` and a working FDN reference card uses it the same way — trust the FDN example.\n71\t- The card's spec (`card_spec.json`) plus the engine source make the behavior obvious. Don't waste a grep on basic things like \"does Flying restrict who can block this\" (the FDN flying cards already show you).\n72\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"75cf876e-0070-4da1-a9bb-823668fe208a","uuid":"ab6e1831-a627-46db-b747-3fd75bfb9d0d","timestamp":"2026-06-10T07:08:46.774Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/skills/grep-rulebook/SKILL.md","content":"---\nname: grep-rulebook\ndescription: Look up Magic: The Gathering rules in /workspace/RULEBOOK.txt using grep. Use whenever you're unsure how a keyword, timing rule, replacement effect, state-based action, or other mechanic actually works — before guessing in a card_impl.py or engine/ change.\n---\n\n`RULEBOOK.txt` (workspace root) is the Magic: The Gathering Comprehensive Rules — the authoritative source for any rules question (keyword behavior, timing, replacement vs trigger ordering, state-based actions, etc.). **Whenever you are unsure how a mechanic works, check the rulebook before guessing.** A wrong card implementation costs you a card; an incorrect-by-design helper in `engine/` can fail many cards at once.\n\n## Read with grep, not cat / Read\n\n`RULEBOOK.txt` is very large. Reading the whole file blows your context budget and returns mostly noise. **Always grep.** Use `Read` only to expand a specific line range you've located with grep.\n\n## File structure (so you know what to grep for)\n\nThe file has two halves:\n\n1. **Numbered rules** (lines ~13–3,860) — hierarchical: section (e.g. `700.`), rule (e.g. `702.2`), subrule (e.g. `702.2a`). Common sections you'll hit a lot:\n   - `100.`–`199.` — game concepts (mana, damage, counters, priority)\n   - `300.`–`399.` — card types (Creature, Instant, Sorcery, Enchantment, Land, Artifact, Planeswalker)\n   - `500.`–`599.` — turn structure (phases, steps, untap, upkeep, combat)\n   - `600.`–`699.` — spells and abilities (casting, resolving, targeting, replacement effects)\n   - `701.` — keyword actions (counter, destroy, exile, sacrifice, regenerate, …)\n   - `702.` — keyword abilities (flying, trample, lifelink, deathtouch, ward, …)\n   - `704.` — state-based actions\n2. **Glossary** (lines ~3,861 onward) — alphabetical term definitions. Often the fastest path for \"what does X mean?\"\n\n## Grep recipes\n\n```bash\n# 1. Look up a keyword ability by name\n#    702. is the keyword-ability section. Most keywords get one numbered subrule.\ngrep -n \"^702\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt | grep -i \"deathtouch\"\n# → 3877:702.2. Deathtouch\n# Then expand:\ngrep -n -A 20 \"^702\\.2\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt | head -30\n\n# 2. Pull a specific rule + its subrules with context\ngrep -n -A 30 \"^704\\.5\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt        # state-based-action enumeration\ngrep -n -A 50 \"^601\\.2\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt        # casting-a-spell steps\n\n# 3. Glossary lookup (case-insensitive, anchored on a line that starts with the term)\n#    Note: only proper keyword abilities have glossary entries. \"Ability words\"\n#    (converge, landfall, magecraft, …) intentionally don't — they're flavor\n#    tags with no rules text. If ^term returns nothing, fall through to (4).\ngrep -n -i \"^deathtouch\" RULEBOOK.txt\ngrep -n -i \"^lifelink\" RULEBOOK.txt\n\n# 4. Find every mention of a mechanic\ngrep -ni \"replacement effect\" RULEBOOK.txt | head -20\ngrep -ni \"ward\" RULEBOOK.txt | head -20\n\n# 5. Find which numbered rule discusses a topic, then drill in\ngrep -n -E \"^[0-9]{3}\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt | grep -i \"combat\"\n# → e.g. 506. Combat Phase\ngrep -n -A 80 \"^506\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt\n```\n\nOnce you've narrowed to a line range, switch to `Read` with `offset` + `limit` to pull just that block (cheaper than re-grepping).\n\n## Best practices\n\n- **Anchor with `^`** on rule-number lookups (`^702\\.`, `^704\\.5\\.`). Without the anchor you'll match every cross-reference to those rules elsewhere in the doc.\n- **Escape the dot** in rule numbers (`702\\.2\\.`) — grep treats `.` as any char. With BRE this matches anyway, but the explicit escape is unambiguous.\n- **Start narrow, then widen.** Try the specific rule number first (`702.19` for Trample). If you don't know the number, search the glossary (`^trample`). If neither hits, search the body with `-i`.\n- **Use `-A` / `-B` for context.** Rules are paragraph-shaped; `-A 20` typically captures a rule plus its first few subrules without overflowing context.\n- **Cap the output with `| head -N`** on broad searches — `grep -i \"creature\"` returns hundreds of hits.\n- **Cross-reference, don't reinvent.** Card rules text often paraphrases the rulebook; when in doubt, the numbered rule wins over your reading of the card.\n\n## When NOT to consult the rulebook\n\n- The behavior is already encoded in `engine/` and a working FDN reference card uses it the same way — trust the FDN example.\n- The card's spec (`card_spec.json`) plus the engine source make the behavior obvious. Don't waste a grep on basic things like \"does Flying restrict who can block this\" (the FDN flying cards already show you).\n","numLines":72,"startLine":1,"totalLines":72}}}
